A raw SRAM PUF gives you a key that changes slightly every boot. Code-offset construction fixes that: enroll once, store a syndrome, reconstruct by correcting.
Parameters: 2 047 raw cells, BCH(2047, 639, t=128) over GF(2¹¹), primitive poly ￼. Corrects 128 errors (5 % BER = ~102 errors — 26 % headroom). 1 408-bit syndrome as helper data. 639-bit codeword, SHA-256 privacy-amplified to a 256-bit UDS.
Security note that matters: helper data is public and leaks nothing about the key given the code structure — but it does mean an attacker with helper data and a noisy measurement of the same die reduces the search space. Entropy budget: 2 047 cells × ~0.42 bits/cell measured min-entropy ≈ 860 bits, minus 1 408 syndrome bits = negative if taken naively. Resolution: the syndrome is derived from the noise, not the key — actual leak is ~639 − 256 = 383 bits of the codeword, still ≥ 256 remaining. Measure the real min-entropy with SP 800-90B on 30 dies before trusting this; the numbers are die-specific.
